{
  "about": "The way in (collective-belfry docs/membership.md \"Applying from the chat (verified)\" and \"A name and a username\"): a belfry's start screen is Log in and a smaller Apply to join. The app makes a key (the applicant key) and signs every call with it (NIP-98, belfry.<domain>/api/v1/…). cases: an answer of `call` (status + body) and `screen`, what the app shows next: code (the six boxes), signed_in (store the session bound to the applicant key like any sign-in; `nip05` = the address said, only when it is name@<domain>), pending (\"Your request is pending\"; `pending` = what /apply/status gives back), unknown (\"No account here yet\" → Apply to join, sent with `proof`), declined, start (the belfry's start screen), log_in (Log in with the address filled in), sign_in_instead (the account holds its own key: the older sign-in with its address), without_proof (ask /apply/start again without the proof: a code is mailed), stay (the refusal `message` on the screen that asked; `clears_code`: the digits typed are of no use), older (this belfry lacks these calls: the native sign-in and the unproven form). `message` is the app's own fixed words, never the server's text. /welcome answering already_set, own_key, not_member or unverified means there is nothing for this person to pick: the chat, no message (signed_in). An /apply/status state we do not know is read as none (start): that call runs on its own, so it never shows an error. A signed_in whose bunker_url is not a bunker address or whose pubkey is not 64 hex is never stored. Suggestions are kept only when they are usernames by the server's rule, three at most. username_check: the line under \"Pick a username:\" for an answer of POST /username/check. A taken username offers up to three suggestions, in the server's order, each one tap (`offers`); the line names them: \"Try a\", \"Try a or b\", \"Try a, b or c\".",
